<think> Okay, so I have this problem here: Given a regular 15-sided polygon with a side length of \(2a\). I need to find the area of the annulus bounded by the circles inscribed in and circumscribed around the 15-sided polygon. Hmm, let me try to visualize this. A regular 15-sided polygon, also known as a pentadecagon, is a polygon with 15 equal sides and 15 equal angles. An annulus is the region between two concentric circles, so in this case, the annulus would be the area between the circle that's inscribed in the polygon (the incircle) and the circle that's circumscribed around the polygon (the circumcircle). So, basically, I need to find the area between these two circles. To find the area of the annulus, I know that the area is equal to the area of the larger circle (the circumcircle) minus the area of the smaller circle (the incircle). So, if I can find the radii of both the incircle and the circumcircle of the regular 15-sided polygon with side length \(2a\), then I can compute the areas of the two circles and subtract them to get the area of the annulus. 

