Now, for the inradius \(r\), which is the apothem, in that same right triangle, the tangent of the angle \(\frac{\pi}{n}\) is opposite over adjacent, which is \(\frac{s/2}{r}\), so \(\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right) = \frac{s/2}{r}\), which I can rearrange to solve for \(r\): \(r = \frac{s/2}{\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)} = \frac{s}{2 \tan\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)}\). Yep, that checks out. So, I have the formulas for both \(R\) and \(r\) in terms of the side length \(s\) and the number of sides \(n\). 

In this problem, the polygon is a regular 15-sided polygon, so \(n = 15\), and the side length \(s\) is given as \(2a\). So, plugging these values into the formulas, the radius \(R\) of the circumcircle is \(R = \frac{2a}{2 \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{15}\right)} = \frac{a}{\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{15}\right)}\), and the radius \(r\) of the incircle is \(r = \frac{2a}{2 \tan\left(\frac{\pi}{15}\right)} = \frac{a}{\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{15}\right)}\). So, now I have expressions for both \(R\) and \(r
